{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_09b2f4ca228b",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 8726,
      "mechanism": "The discovery trace shows m_axis_tvalid is 0 at cycles 4–5 when the reference expects 1. In axis_switch, each source interface (s_ifaces) maintains select_reg, drop_reg, and select_valid_reg that drive int_axis_tvalid and int_s_axis_tready. These three registers have initial values in the declaration but no synchronous reset assignments in the always @(posedge clk) block (lines 220–229). On the first cycles, if the simulator does not honor the declaration initializers, the registers can power up with arbitrary values, corrupting the routing decision and preventing the downstream arbiter from issuing a valid grant. The patch adds synchronous resets for drop_reg and select_reg so that the input-side state machine starts from a known state, enabling the first valid transaction to propagate to the output and match the expected m_axis_tvalid at cycle 4."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c1a2f86ca667",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 8726,
      "mechanism": "Same symptom as the previous entry: m_axis_tvalid is 0 instead of 1 at cycles 4–5. The s_ifaces always block (lines 220–229) resets select_valid_reg but leaves select_reg and drop_reg without a reset assignment. If the simulator ignores declaration initializers, select_reg may be X, causing the (int_s_axis_tvalid[m] && select_valid_reg && !drop_reg) << select_reg expression to produce X/0 and the ready logic (int_axis_tready[select_reg*M_COUNT+m] || drop_reg) to misbehave. This patch resets select_reg in the clocked block, complementing the reset of drop_reg and select_valid_reg so the whole input-side state machine is deterministically initialized, which is sufficient to unblock the first grant and produce the expected output valid."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_35cdbadc2436",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 8726,
      "mechanism": "m_axis_tvalid stuck at 0 for the first two expected assertion cycles. The s_ifaces state machine uses drop_reg to suppress int_axis_tvalid until the packet tail (tlast) is seen. Without a reset, drop_reg can power up asserted, permanently disabling the path from source to arbiter. This patch adds a synchronous reset for drop_reg only. If the root cause is solely drop_reg starting high, this change alone restores the first grant and fixes m_axis_tvalid at cycle 4."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_bcd660a781f3",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 9113,
      "mechanism": "The source-side bit-slice int_axis_tvalid[m*M_COUNT +: M_COUNT] uses M_COUNT as the part-select width. With the test parameters (S_COUNT=4, M_COUNT=1) the expression is correct, but the port mismatch appears on the first grants. If the elaboration-time value of M_COUNT differs from the simulation-time value used by the reference, the slice width would be wrong and the valid bit would land in the wrong position or be dropped. Replacing M_COUNT with the compile-time constant CL_M_COUNT makes the slice width immune to parameter mismatches and guarantees the single valid bit is placed at bit 0 of the slice, allowing the arbiter request to be formed correctly."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_30724f58bcce",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 9113,
      "mechanism": "Identical slice-width issue as the previous candidate but only one site is changed. The int_axis_tvalid concatenation for each source uses a variable part-select width (M_COUNT). If M_COUNT is not a constant during elaboration, the generated vector width can be incorrect, causing the arbiter to see a malformed request vector and never assert grant_valid. Using CL_M_COUNT (the $clog2(M_COUNT) constant) fixes the slice width for the test configuration and restores the first valid output."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_d4cc02256aa6",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 11705,
      "mechanism": "In the m_ifaces block, s_axis_tvalid_mux selects the granted source with int_s_axis_tdata[grant_encoded*DATA_WIDTH +: DATA_WIDTH] (correct) but s_axis_tvalid_mux uses int_axis_tvalid[grant_encoded*S_COUNT+n]. The part-select width is S_COUNT, yet the arbiter grant_encoded ranges over S_COUNT sources, so the stride must be M_COUNT (the number of destinations per source). With M_COUNT=1 the bug is latent, but any parameter change would misalign the valid bit. Replacing S_COUNT with CL_M_COUNT corrects the stride so the granted source’s valid bit is read, enabling grant_valid and m_axis_tvalid at cycle 4."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_b00008c7be19",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 9113,
      "mechanism": "Two coordinated slice-width fixes: the source-side int_axis_tvalid slice uses M_COUNT (should be CL_S_COUNT per the patch) and the destination-side s_axis_tvalid_mux slice uses S_COUNT (should be CL_M_COUNT). If both strides are wrong simultaneously, the valid bit is mispositioned twice, cancelling in some configurations but failing in the tested one. Correcting both ensures the request vector construction and the grant-time mux select agree on the bit layout, allowing the first packet to produce m_axis_tvalid=1 at cycle 4."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_8cea8cdcd28f",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 12487,
      "mechanism": "The acknowledge assignment acknowledge[m] = grant[m] && int_axis_tvalid[m*M_COUNT+n] && s_axis_tlast_mux && s_axis_tready_mux uses a variable part-select width M_COUNT. If the elaboration width is incorrect, the acknowledge bit selects the wrong source, the arbiter never releases the grant, and grant_valid stays low. Changing the slice width to CL_M_COUNT makes the acknowledge indexing consistent with the request indexing, letting the first grant complete and m_axis_tvalid to assert on schedule."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The public trace shows the very first expected m_axis_tvalid at cycle 4 is 0 in the DUT. The failure is consistent with either (a) unreset state in the input-side routing state machine (select_reg, drop_reg, select_valid_reg) or (b) incorrect vector part-select widths (M_COUNT vs. S_COUNT vs. their $clog2 constants) that misplace the valid bit during request/grant/acknowledge handshake. The top-ranked patches address the synchronous reset of the input-side registers because the always @(posedge clk) block (lines 220–229) resets only select_valid_reg; if the simulator does not apply declaration initializers, the other two registers power up X and corrupt the first routing decision. The next tier fixes the part-select widths on the source and destination sides so that the single valid bit per source is correctly positioned in the concatenated request/acknowledge vectors, which is required for the round-robin arbiter to issue a valid grant on the first transaction."
}
